{{Politics of Okaiken}}

The '''Ministry of Land, Infrastructure, Transport and Tourism''' ({{Nihongo|国土交通省|Kokudo-kōtsū-shō}}), commonly designated by the abbreviation '''MLIT''', is a cabinet-level ministry of the [[Government of Okaiken]] responsible for comprehensive national land development, civil engineering public works, transport network regulation, maritime safety, aviation administration, and the promotion of tourism. With an annual appropriation of approximately 128 billion Sen—constituting roughly 8 percent of total national expenditure—and a permanent civil service establishment of 67,400 personnel, the ministry represents the second-largest department in the executive branch.

The ministry exercises regulatory command over the Shinkansen bullet train network, the Oka-Pass electronic expressway matrix, commercial maritime ports, civil airports, and major river basins. The incumbent minister is [[Seiran Mochizuki]] of the [[Okaiken Socialist Party]] (OSP), who entered the [[Ikazaki Cabinet]] on 20 May 2026. A former frontline railway worker and veteran organizer within the [[Okaiken Transport Workers Union]] (OTWU), Mochizuki's appointment marked a profound transformation in ministerial priorities, moving from conservative market-driven outsourcing toward aggressive passenger safety enforcement and labor protections headlined by the [[Yamamoto Hina Safety Commission]] and the mandatory Two-Man Mandate.

==Historical development and the construction state==

===Origins and the 2001 mega-merger===

The contemporary ministry is the product of the 2001 Central Government Reform, which amalgamated four historically autonomous administrative organs: the Ministry of Construction (建設省, ''Kensetsu-shō''), the Ministry of Transport (運輸省, ''Un'yu-shō''), the National Land Agency, and regional development administrations. Throughout the post-war high-growth era, the Ministry of Construction dominated domestic political economics through the construction state (土建国家, ''doken kokka''), directing massive river damming, land reclamation, and highway paving programs that underpinned the ruling party's regional electoral machines. Concurrently, the Ministry of Transport managed the post-war modernization of railways and guided the historic 1987 privatization of the national rail system into regional operating entities.

The 2001 consolidation was designed to dismantle departmental silos, harmonizing spatial land-use zoning with regional transport planning. However, administrative critics noted that the colossal bureaucracy remained bifurcated between construction technocrats and transport regulators. During the Aoyagi premiership (2014–2021), the ministry pivoted capital allocations away from speculative rural highway projects toward long-term seismic retrofitting and asset lifecycle maintenance.

===The Nakayama Station crisis and safety overhaul===

Under the subsequent administration of Prime Minister [[Honda Shigetsuma]], the ministry reversed regulatory stringency, promoting one-man driver-only operations (ワンマン運転, ''wanman unten'') on commuter rail lines and incentivizing private operators to outsource mechanical safety inspections to corporate contractors such as [[Kizan Heavy Industries]]. This aggressive deregulation culminated in the March 2026 Nakayama Station dragging incident, in which a high school passenger was killed when automated sensor doors failed and the single-operator crew departed without visual platform verification.

The catastrophe precipitated widespread civil unrest during the March Maelstrom and triggered the fall of the Honda-Nakayama government. Upon assuming the ministerial portfolio in the Ikazaki Cabinet, Seiran Mochizuki executed Day One executive directives: establishing the [[Yamamoto Hina Safety Commission]] as an autonomous rail oversight authority with statutory grounding powers, repealing one-man operational licenses on all heavily traveled passenger lines, and mandating the Two-Man Crew Rule across all heavy rail corridors.

